## 1. Introduction
Hydrogen energy as green energy with high energy density and zero carbon emission is deemed to be one of the most possible alternatives to traditional fossil energy in the 21st century [1,2]. A large scale of electrocatalytic overall water splitting (OWS) technology to produce sustainable green hydrogen has been extensively studied on account of the enormous reserves of water on the earth and pollutant-free [3]. At present, the OWS can be divided into two half-reactions - hydrogen evolution (HER) and oxygen evolution (OER), the commercial catalysts of which are usually precious metal – Pt-based and Ru/Ir-based catalytic electrode materials, respectively [4]. However, its high price and low reserves limit its wide application in the industry. Therefore, researchers have been devoting a lot of effort to developing alternative OWS catalysts with high catalytic activity, high durability, and competitive price [5,6].
To solve these problems and achieve better catalytic performance, various strategies were developed [12]. For instance, Stern et al. [13] synthesized a core–shell $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{N i}\mathsf{O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ , which was applied to the HER and OER bifunctional catalysts, and assembled it into an electrolytic water electrode to realize the electrolytic water decomposition at a lower voltage. Zhang et al. [14] prepared $\mathsf{C o P}/\$ ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ nanoparticles implanted in a hollow porous $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon polyhedron, which realizes OWS by electrocatalysis under different pH values. Because this phosphide not only has a unique porous structure and provides more active sites, but also can adjust the electronic structure of the catalyst. Wu et al. [15] designed a heterogeneous ${\sf N i}_{2}{\sf P-}{\sf F e}_{2}{\sf P}$ microchip, which achieved OWS under high current density and maintained its durability in seawater, indicating that this phosphide material possesses excellent corrosion resistance. However, the metal phosphide will still occur serious agglomeration and detachment, resulting in unsatisfactory stability in the long-term operation process.
To stabilize the structure of the material, avoid agglomeration, and increase its electrical conductivity, an effective strategy is to select the precursor with a stable crystal structure and a large specific surface area, and pyrolysis carbonization to form a composite material of phosphide and carbon [16,17]. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are considered to be one of the most suitable precursors for designing various nanohybrids for the field of catalytic conversion of energy [18–20]. In this regard, materials scientists have developed a variety of different nanostructures, including nanoclusters [21,22], nanowires [23–25], nanotubes [26–28], nanosheets [29–32], nanoarrays [33,34], etc. These materials need to modify the active components on the conductive interface through the adhesive, so as to cause the active sites to be covered, resulting in the active sites being covered and a large agglomeration of active components. Therefore, the in-situ growth of active substances on self-supporting electrode materials can effectively avoid the accumulation of active substances [35]. The active substance is directly loaded on the self-supporting electrode, such as copper foil, titanium foil, carbon cloth, nickel foam, etc [36,37]. However, most of these works are limited to onedimensional and two-dimensional materials. It has been rarely reported that three-dimensional spherical nanostructures are anchored on self-supporting bifunctional electrodes. As we know, most electrocatalytic reactions occur at surface locations, so it is a key issue to expose more active sites on the surface of the electrode material. To solve this problem, it is an effective strategy to regulate multi-dimensional interface of the electrode material, which plays an important role in electron transport and gas mass transfer and is conducive to promoting the electrocatalytic process.
Herein, we propose a three-dimensional interface structure of MOF-derived nanospheres and nanoflowers, which exposes more active sites and facilitates rapid electron transfer. Specifically, the Ni-1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid (Ni-BTC) MOF-derived phosphide material ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P@C}}$ anchored on self-supporting electrode material and systematically studied the effect of phosphide converted at different growth times of Ni-BTC MOF on the electrocatalytic performance. As the time of solvothermal in-situ growth gradually prolonged, Ni-BTC MOF further transforms from an irregular crystal nucleus into a smooth nanosphere and further formed nanoflower-shaped nanospheres. The subsequently pyrolysis phosphating and carbonization transform the Ni-BTC MOFs sample into carbon-anchored metal ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ active material nanoflower $(\mathsf{N i}_{2^{-}}$ $\mathsf{P}@\mathsf{C}-3\mathrm{~h~}$ , $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/\Nu\mathrm{F-6}\ h$ , $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F-}12\ \mathrm{h},$ , and $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-24\ h$ , respectively). The nanospheres anchored on the nickel foam substrate present a convex interface that is conducive to the sufficient contact of electrolyte, while the nanoflowers emerge a layered interface that contributes to the exposure of active sites. This composite material with multiple interfaces needs low overpotentials of only $69\mathrm{mV}$ and $205\mathrm{mV}$ to drive a current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for HER and OER, respectively, in $1.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH. As for OWS, $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}@\mathsf{C}/$ NF-12 h electrode realizes an ultralow cell voltage of $1.55~\mathrm{V}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ which is comparable to the catalytic activity of the fiducial $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf C/||\mathsf{R u O}_{2}$ . The co-existing nanoflower and nanosphere composites of $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-12\ h$ provide more site for multiple interface reactions and possess massive exposed active sites with extremely low charge transfer impedance, which allows for the improved contact of electrolytes, leading to superior catalytic performance. This work offers general guidance for the design of highly efficient self-supporting transition-metal-based OWS electrocatalysts.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Chemicals
All the chemicals are analytical grade. Hydrochloric acid (HCl), N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF), potassium hydroxide (KOH), methanol, and ethanol were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd (Beijing, China). Sodium hypophosphite $(\mathsf{N a H}_{2}.$ - ${\mathsf{P O}}_{2}{\mathrm{.}}$ ), Polyvinylpyrrolidone K30 (PVP), 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid were purchased from Aladdin Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd (Shanghai, China). Pt/C $20~\mathrm{wt\%})$ and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ were purchased from Alfa Aesar (Heysham, Britain). Nickel foam $(99.99\%$ purity, $1.5~\mathrm{mm}$ thickness, $0.45~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ pore size.) was purchased from Kunshan Shengshijing New Materials Co., Ltd (Kunshan, China).
## 2.2. Synthesis of Ni-BTC/NF
NF $(2\ \mathrm{cm}\ \times4\ \mathrm{cm})$ was ultrasonicated in ethyl alcohol, acetone, and hydrochloric acid for $15\mathrm{min}$ to remove the oxide layer on the surface. In particular, a certain amount of PVP (0, 1, 2, 3, 4 g), $0.3{\mathrm{~g~}}$ of 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid, and $0.864\mathrm{~g~}$ of ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}.6{\sf H}_{2}0$ were mixed with the solvent containing $20~\mathrm{mL}$ distilled water, $20~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol, and 20 mL N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF) respectively and stirred for $30\mathrm{min}$ to form a uniform green solution. Subsequently, the mixture was poured into a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ Teflon autoclave, and the nickel foam was placed in the solution, sealed, and heated to $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to maintain the in-situ growth reaction for 3 h, 6 h, $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , $24\mathrm{h}$ , which was labeled as Ni-BTC/NF-3 h, Ni-BTC/NF-6 h, Ni-BTC/ NF-12 h, Ni-BTC/NF-24 h, respectively.
## 2.3. Synthesis of $N i_{2}P@C/N F$ electrode materials
## 2.4. Characterization of electrode materials
The crystalline phase composition of the samples was characterized by X-ray diffraction (XRD, Bruker D8 Advance). The microstructure of the electrode materials was measured by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Hitachi SU8010, Japan). Ultrafine structure and lattice fringes were tested by transmission electron microscopy (TEM, Tecnai G2 F20, America). Raman spectra of various electrode materials were characterized by a Raman spectrometer (Raman, Horiba Xplora Plus, French). The surface morphology and state of the catalyst were tested was characterized by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, VG Scientific ESCALAB MK II, Britain). The prepared self-supporting electrode materials were assembled into the equipment of a tablet press, which was pressed into a flat sheet. The specific surface area and characterization distribution of samples were tested by ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms (Micromeritics ASAP 2020). The contact angle of the material in $1.0\ \mathrm{M}$ KOH electrolyte was measured by the trapped droplet method using the Kruss DSA20 system (Hamburg, Germany).
## 2.5. Electrochemical measurements
The electrochemical properties (HER, OER, and OWS) of the electrode catalyst materials were performed by an Electrochemical workstation (CHI760E electrochemical workstation, CH Instruments, Inc., Shanghai, China) on a standard three-electrode system, in which self-supporting materials $(1\times1~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ were served as the working electrode, $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ electrode was selected as the reference electrode, and graphite rod was applied as the counter electrode. The prepared self-supporting materials were directly used as catalytic electrodes without Nafion titration while $\sf P t/C$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ were the reference samples which needed the bonding effect of Nafion because of its powder materials. Specifically, $5.0\mathrm{mg}$ of the control sample was dispersed in a mixed solution containing $250~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ethanol, $250~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ deionized water and $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Nafion $(5\%)$ , and ultrasound acted for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ to form a uniform catalyst ink. Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) was tested from $0.1\mathrm{\V}$ to $-0.6\:\mathrm{V}$ (vs. reversible hydrogen electrode, RHE) for HER and from $1.0\mathrm{V}$ to $2.0\mathrm{V}$ (vs. RHE) for OER in 1.0 M KOH $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{pH}=14\$ ) at a scan rate of $5\mathrm{m}\mathrm{V}s^{-1}$ and iR (80 $\%$ ) compensation. The Tafel evaluated by steady state polarization method was to obtain the current density at the 200 s of chronoamperometric (CA) response at an interval potential of $0.050\mathrm{\:V}$ in the catalytic turnover area (the range of HER is $-0.02$ $6\sim-0.476\:\mathrm{V}$ , and the range of OER is $1.424\sim1.924\:\mathrm{V};$ . The Tafel slope of the steady state polarization curve was calculated by using the current density in the steady state and the corresponding overpotential compensated by the resistance. Cyclic voltammogram (CV) was collected between $0.425\mathrm{~V~}$ and $0.625\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs. RHE) with various scan rates from $50\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ to $500\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ . Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was collected in the potentiation mode at a potential of $-0.15\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs. RHE) from $10^{-1}~\mathrm{Hz}$ to $10^{5}~\mathrm{Hz}$ . The amplitude of the alternating voltage was a potential of  0.1 7 V versus RHE for HER and $1.58{\mathrm{V}}{}$ versus RHE for OER. The electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) was obtained by recording double-layer capacitance $\big(\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}\big)$ values by cyclic voltammetry in the Faradaic potential region from 0.425 to $0.625\mathrm{~V~}$ at different scan rates $(50\sim500~\mathrm{mV}~s^{-1},$ .
Calculation of the number of active sites: The active site (n) of the catalytic electrode was measured by CV in $1.0\ \mathrm{M}$ KOH electrolyte $\mathrm{(pH}=14,$ at a scan rate of $50\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ with potential ranging from 0.8 to $1.6\:\mathrm{V}$ for OER and 0.2 to $0.6\mathrm{V}$ for HER (VS RHE). The area of the integral curve can be used to calculate the absolute value of the voltammetric charge. The equation $(\pmb{S_{1}})$ was used to calculate the number of active sites (n):
$$
n=\frac{Q}{m F}=\frac{I t}{m F}=\frac{I V}{m F\upsilon}
$$
where $\boldsymbol{Q}$ is the total voltammetric charge and $F$ is the faraday constant $(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ , $I$ is the current (A), t is the time (s) and $V$ is the potential (volt, V) and $v$ is the scan rate $(\mathsf{V}\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ .
Calculation of turnover frequency (TOF): The TOF for OER and HER was determined using equation (S2):
$$
\mathrm{TOF}={\frac{j A}{n m F}}
$$
where $j=$ current in ampere (A) obtained during LSV measurement, $A=$ area of the electrode $\cdot1~\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ , $F=$ Faraday constant (96485 C $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ ), $m=$ Number of electrons transferred to generate one molecule of the product ( $m=4$ for OER and $m=2$ for HER) and $n=$ Number of active sites catalyzing the reactions respectively $(\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\cdot$ ).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Fabrication and structure characterization
Scheme 1 displays the preparation synthetic route of Ni-BTC MOF with reaction time and its corresponding derivatives of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}$ - $\mathsf{P}@{\mathsf{C}}/\mathsf{N F}$ nanosphere. Ni-BTC MOF was prepared on nickel foam substrate by solvothermal precipitation method. A small quantity of organic ligand (Trimesic acid) and stabilizing agent (polyvinylpyrrolidone, PVP) was coordinated with unsaturated nickel ion centers to form homogeneous spherical MOF (Ni-BTC) in a solvent containing deionized water, ethanol, and DMF at a ratio of 1:1:1 [38]. The accurately different growth time $(3\ \mathrm{h},6\ \mathrm{h}$ · $12\ \mathrm{h},24\ \mathrm{h}$ ) was taken to better understand and optimize this the evolutionary process. Ni-BTC nanosphere was further tightly anchored on the foam substrate to formulate $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ by phosphating at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with ${\sf N a H}_{2}{\sf P O}_{2}$ as phosphorus source, which solves the problem that the nanospheres are not strong on nickel metal substrates.
To explore the morphological evolution of Ni-BTC MOF, the microscopic morphologies of different time hydrothermal samples were provided by SEM. As shown in Fig. 1a(i-ii), Ni-BTC-3 h exhibits irregular and incomplete spherical with diameters of about $1.2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ attached to the surface of the substrate, indicating that the crystal initially starts nucleation. As the growth time increases, Ni-BTC slowly grows along the crystal nucleus under stable hydrothermal conditions for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ , in which the particle size of the microspheres increases to about $2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Fig. 1b(i-ii)). As the hydrothermal reaction reaches $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , an archetypal Ostwald maturation process further emerges to yield regular smooth-faced nanospheres with diameters of about $2.5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. 1c(i-ii)). After $24\mathrm{h}$ of reaction, the Ni-MOF-24 h sample is further grown to form the nanospheres of the surface with a certain degree of roughness (Fig. 1d(i-ii)). Afterward, Ni-BTC MOFs were pyrolyzed and phosphated at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the Ar atmosphere to acquire carbon-coated nickel phosphide nanospheres $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F})$ attached to the nickel foam substrate, showing a completely different morphology (Fig. 1- e-h). For the solid material $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{N F}{-}3\mathrm{~h~}$ (Fig. 1e) and $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{N F}{-}6\mathrm{~h~}$ (Fig. 1f), the carbonized and agglomerated spheroids appear in the regional area. After $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ of reaction, the pyrolyzed nanosphere shows a lamellar nanoflower-like morphology nanospheres structure on the surface, which exposes more active sites (Fig. 1g). It is also observed that rough nanoclusters agglomerated on the outer surface of the nanospheres in the $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-24\ h$ sample (Fig. 1h).
The obtained $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-12\ln$ material was dispersed into ethanol solution by ultrasonic and then characterized by TEM to analyze its composition and structure. It is further proved that $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-12$ h shows the morphology of nanoflowers/- nanospheres, and the rough surface of its nanospheres presents a nanowire array of about $15\sim25~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. 2a-d). The scanning transmission electron microscopy (HADDF-STEM) reveals the good lattice fringes (Fig. 2e), corresponding to the (110) crystal and (111) crystal of the ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ with the interplanar spacings of 2.93 and $2.21\mathring{\mathsf{A}},$ , respectively, which is in agreement with the XRD pattern [39–41]. Furthermore, high-angle annular dark-field TEM e nergy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (HAADF-TEM-EDS) elemental mapping can confirm the presence of Ni, P, C elements in the $\mathbf{Ni}_{2}$ - $\mathsf{P}@{\mathsf{C}}$ spheres microspheres, implying the complete phosphating process (Fig. 2f-i).
To verify the crystallinity of Ni-BTC MOFs at different growth stages, XRD analysis was carried out in Fig. 3a. After hydrothermal treatment for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ , there is basically no characteristic peak of NiBTC, indicating that the material is still nucleation stage and possessed no complete crystal structure. With the extension of hydrothermal time, Ni-BTC continues to grow along the core, and its crystalline derivative peak gradually emerges. Ni-BTC- $.12\mathrm{~h~}$ NiBTC MOF is basically complete. The XRD diffraction of Ni-BTCderived $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ materials with different growth times is shown in the Fig. 3b. The XRD crystals of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F{-}3}\mathrm{~h~}$ are not quite complete, showing that phosphating is not complete possibly because of the incomplete nucleation of Ni-BTC while the $\mathbf{Ni}_{2}$ - $\mathsf{P}@\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{N F}{-}12\mathrm{~h~}$ crystals exhibit complete ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ crystals, which are shown at $40.8^{\circ}$ , $44.6^{\circ}$ , $47.3^{\circ}$ , 54.2, $54.9^{\circ},72.7^{\circ}$ , and $74.7^{\circ}$ , which can be corresponding to (111), (20 1), (210), (300), (211), (311) and (400) of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ (JPCDS No. 74-1385), respectively [42,43]. This indicates that Ni-BTC/NF can be successfully converted to $\mathsf{N i}_{2}$ - $\mathsf{P}@{\mathsf{C}}/\mathsf{N F}$ after phosphate and calcination. Fig. 3c shows the Raman spectra of the $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-3\ h$ $^\textrm{\scriptsize6h}$ , $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ emerges 1341 (D peak) and $1571\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (G peak) which corresponds to the disordered carbon with defect structure and the conductive graphitic carbon, respectively. Notably, with the increase of Ni-BTC hydrothermal time, the $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ ratio declines from 1.01 to 0.87, indicating that the nucleation growth time of Ni-BTC MOF is sufficiently favorable for the formation of graphitic carbon. The chemical states of Ni and P on the surface of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ spheres were further studied by XPS (Fig. 3d-f). The Ni 2p spectrum of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P/N F}$ shows four characteristic peaks, corresponding to the spin–orbit splitting values of Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ $(873.3\ \mathrm{eV})$ and Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ $855.5~\mathrm{eV})$ of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , two satellite peaks at 879.3 and 861.0 eV.[44] The XPS spectrum of $\mathsf{N i}\ 2\mathsf{p}$ of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{N P C}/\mathsf{N F}-12\mathrm{~h~}$ is divided into six peaks: Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ $872.5{\ \mathrm{eV}})$ and Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ $853.9~\mathrm{eV})$ of metallic ${\mathsf{N i}}^{0}$ , Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ $(873.8~\mathrm{eV})$ and Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ $855.8~\mathrm{eV})$ of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , two satellite peaks at 880.0 and $861.3\ \mathrm{eV}.$ . Interestingly, ${\mathsf{N i}}^{0}$ appears on the surface of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ . This is mainly due to the carbonization accompanying the phosphating process at high temperature, which is conducive to the reduction of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ into Ni element. It may also be due to the protection of carbon layer, which prevents some nickel metals from phosphating [45].
Regarding the spectrum of $\mathtt{P2p}$ of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ , the two characteristic peaks at $133.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to $\mathsf{P{\mathrm{-}}C}$ bond, while the peak at $134.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to $\mathtt{P-O}$ bond, which is due to the oxidation of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}@\mathsf{C}$ exposed to air caused [46]. The C 1 s spectrum of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ shows four characteristic peaks, at $284.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ , $285.5\ \mathrm{eV}.$ , and $286.6\ \mathrm{eV}.$ , corresponding to the spin–orbit splitting values of CAC, CAO, CAP. As shown in the Fig. 3d-f, with the extension of crystallization time, the two peaks of Ni 2p, P 2p, and C 1 s show an obvious positive shift of $0.8~\mathrm{eV}$ , a negative shift of $0.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $0.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ respectively, indicating that the electron transfer from Ni to P and C is enhanced on the surface. P 2p shows that the more pronounced electron transfer in ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P@C}}$ is due to the interaction between ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ and carbon layers in the closely contacted heterointerface. In addition, Ni is both partially positive $\left({\delta}^{+}\right)$ and P is partially negative $\left(\delta^{-}\right)$ , which together with Ni $\left({\delta}^{+}\right)$ and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{P}}(\delta^{-})$ as hydride acceptor and proton acceptor centers is beneficial for the HER process [47,48].
In addition, the adsorption desorption isotherms of the samples show a hierarchical pore structure and typical IV-type curves (Fig. 3g). The low-pressure area possesses a hysteresis which proves the existence of micropores while the high-pressure area has strong adsorption capacity corresponding to mesopores, indicating that ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P@C}}$ the materials all show the coexistence of micropores and mesopores. The pore size distribution in Fig. 3h further confirms the existence of microporous and mesoporous pores, in which micropores can provide more active sites for ion adsorption, while mesopores play an important role in providing fast ion transport channels and gas mass transfer. With the increase of the growth time of MOF crystal, the specific surface area of the ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}@C$ presents from $79.2~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C}-3\mathsf{h})$ , increases to $113.4~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C-12\ h})$ and then drops to $105.6\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C}-24\mathsf{h})$ 1 (Fig. 3i). Obviously, $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C}\cdot12\mathrm{~h~}$ has the largest specific surface area, mainly because the crystal growth at this time forms a complete nanospheres/nanoflowers, and the transitional growth will lead to the aggregation of the morphology, resulting in the decrease of the specific surface area, such as $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C}\mathsf{-}24\mathrm{~h~}$ .
Furthermore, the wettability of the catalyst is crucial for the water electrolysis reaction. A contact angle was measured to analyze the wetting properties of the prepared material in the KOH solution. Compared with NF $(78^{\circ})$ , Ni-BTC/NF $(38^{\circ})$ , $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ shows excellent hydrophilic properties $(14^{\circ})$ , which can increase the contact area between electrolyte and electrode material, thereby enhancing the performance of water splitting (Fig. 3j-l).
## 3.2. Evaluation of hydrogen evolution performance
The electrocatalytic HER activity of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}@\mathsf{C}$ electrodes was evaluated using a standard three-electrode system on the CHI760E electrochemical workstation in a $1.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH electrolyte. Commercial $\sf P t/C$ , nickel foam, Ni-BTC/NF, $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P/N F}$ , $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F{-}}3\mathrm{h}$ , $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}@\mathsf{C}/$ NF-6 h, $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-12\ h$ , and $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F-}24\mathrm{~h~}$ with the same surface area of $1.0~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ were tested and compared. Commercial $\sf P t/C$ , as a comparison, possesses the best HER activity and $52~\mathrm{mV}$ (vs. RHE) can drive a current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , which is challenging to surpass. As can be seen from Fig. 4a-b, as expected, the $\mathsf{N i}_{2}$ - $\mathsf{P}@\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{N F}{-}12\mathrm{~h~}$ nanospheres exhibit excellent performance for HER, with an overpotential of $69~\mathrm{mV}$ (vs. RHE) at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ , which is superior to that of nickel foam, Ni-BTC/NF, $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P/N F}$ , $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}@\mathsf{C}/$ NF-3 h, $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F–6~h}$ , $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@/N F{-}}24\mathrm{~h~}$ , respectively are $171~\mathrm{mV}$ $158~\mathrm{mV}$ , $131~\mathrm{mV}$ , $109~\mathrm{{mV}}$ , $95~\mathrm{mV}$ and $75~\mathrm{mV}$ (vs. RHE). However, $\sf P t/C$ has no more advantages, in which $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-12\ h$ exhibits excellent performance at high current density $(>135\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}.$ ). The remarkable electrocatalytic performance of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ nanospheres outperforms previously reported HER performance (Table S1).
The Tafel plot calculated from LSV can reflect the kinetic mechanism of HER, which can directly comprehend the intrinsic catalytic performance of electrocatalysts. As shown in Fig. 4c, the Tafel slope of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C-12~h}$ is fitted to be $63.8~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ , positively correlated with the catalytic performance of HER, which is significantly better than that of NF $(188.8~\mathrm{\mV~\dec^{-1}}.$ , Ni-BTC/NF $(155.2~\mathrm{~mV~}\mathrm{~dec}^{-1}.$ , $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P/N F}$ $\cdot148.6~\mathrm{\mV}~\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ ), $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F{-}}3$ h $\mathrm{^{'}131.2~m V~d e c^{-1}}.$ , $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@\cal C/N F{-}6$ h $(97.8\ \mathrm{\mV\dec^{-1}}$ ), $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C_{\varepsilon}}$ NF2 $\mathrm{4~h~}(67.8\mathrm{~mV~dec}^{-1},$ , etc. To react the HER catalytic kinetics more truly, the Tafel slope was calculated by the constant potential steady state polarization method [49]. Specifically, the current density at the $200~s$ of chronoamperometric (CA) response at an interval potential of $0.050\mathrm{V}$ in the catalytic turnover area $_{(-0.026}$ $\sim-0.476\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE) (Fig. S1a). The Tafel slope of the steady state polarization curve was calculated by using the current density in the steady state and the corresponding overpotential compensated by the resistance (Fig. S1b-c). According to this method, we tested the HER LSV curves of the prepared materials by the steady state polarization method and the steady state Tafel slopes was calculated as shown in Fig. 4d-f. The Tafel slope change law of prepared materials by different growth times are basically the same, but it is probably higher than $6\sim25\:\mathrm{mV}\:\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ than the instantaneous Tafel. These indicate that the active phase metal phosphide $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P})$ of the connected nanospheres plays an important role in the fast reaction kinetics of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P@C}}$ in the rate-determining Volmer process of alkaline HER. It is worth noting that the HER performance of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}@\mathsf{C}/$ NF-12 h is superior to that of others, owing to Ni-BTC MOFsderived carbon-framework nanoflowers/nanospheres riveted to nickel foam. Such three-dimensional interconnected nanospheres not only provide abundant charge transfer pathways for fast electron transfer but also expose more intrinsically active sites, which facilitate the efficient mass transfer of electrolytes and gaseous products.
Furthermore, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) analysis was performed on the obtained materials to explore the electron transport kinetics of the electrode materials, which were directly derived from the Nyquist plots (Fig. $\mathbf{4g}{\dot{\mathbf{\eta}}}$ . $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-12\ h$ exhibits the smallest charge transfer resistance $\mathtt{R}=8$ ohm) compared to the comparative samples $\mathit{\Delta}\cdot\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{NF}}=40$ ohm, $\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{Ni}}$ -BTC/ $_{\mathrm{NF}}=34$ ohm, $\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{Ni2P/NF-3h}}=25$ ohm, $\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{Ni2P@C/NF-3h}}=20$ ohm, RNi2P@C/ $_{\mathrm{NF-Gh}}=16$ ohm, $\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{Ni2P@C/NF-12h}}=\ 14\ \mathrm{{ot}}$ hm), indicating that the insitu growth of Ni-BTC-derived $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-12\ h$ on a nickel foam substrate shows excellent electron conduction performance.
To further appraise the stability of the $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-12\ln$ the current density has no obvious attenuation after $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ continuous chronoamperometry test (Fig. 4h). At the same time, scarcely any change in the LVS curve was observed before and after a continuous 3000 CV (Fig. 4i). The samples tested by CV were characterized by SEM (Figure S12) and TEM images (Figure S13), which shows that the nanospheres/nanoflowers structure remains basically unaffected. Further Raman spectrum characterization shows that the change of $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ decreased slightly which represents a slight reduction of carbon defects (Figure S16). Meanwhile, there is little difference in peak separation between $\mathsf{N i}_{2\mathsf{p}}$ and $\mathsf{P}_{2\mathsf{p}}$ (Figure S17a, b). These characterization results confirms that the MOF-derived $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-12\ln$ exhibits excellent stability of HER, making industrial research for long-term mass production of hydrogen. This self-supporting electrocatalyst has significant advantages of longterm durability due to the arrays of nanospheres which can effectively prevent the blocking of active sites and the shedding of catalysts.
## 3.3. Evaluation of oxygen evolution performance
The OER catalytic performances of synthetic samples were evaluated in an alkaline solution (1 M KOH) by using a three-electrode electrochemical system. The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves of OER in Fig. 5a indicate that the overpotential needs to drive $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ in the sequence of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C-12}\mathrm{~h~}$ ( $205~\mathrm{mV}$ vs. RHE) $<{\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{P@C}}{-6}$ h $221~\mathrm{mV}$ vs. RHE) $<\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C}-24~\mathrm{h}$ $213~\mathrm{mV}$ vs. RHE) $<\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C}.-3$ h $268~\mathrm{mV}$ vs. RHE) $\angle\cdot\angle$ Ni-BTC/NF $289~\mathrm{mV}$ vs. RHE) $<\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P/N F}$ $305~\mathrm{{\mV}}$ vs. RHE) $\angle<$ nickel foam $354~\mathrm{mV}$ vs. RHE). The polarization curve of OER LSV is obtained by forward scanning (Fig. 5d), which is also consistent with the LSV performance law obtained by reverse scanning. No matter forward or reverse scanning, reconstruction peak appears in the polarization curve, which is due to the reconstruction phenomenon formed by the conversion of active substance ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ into NiOOH [50]. Fig. 5b shows the comparison of the working overpotentials of different catalysts at current densities of 10, 100, and $200\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , demonstrating that $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C-12}\mathrm{~h~}$ possesses obvious advantages over $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}/\mathsf{N F}$ under the condition of high current. In addition, the catalytic performance of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ can also reach an advanced level among the recently reported $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}$ catalysts (Table S2). Fig. 5c is the Tafel slope of the catalytic material calculated from the LSV data. The Tafel slope of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ is only $63.0~\mathrm{mV}~\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ , which is smaller than that of NF ( $152.5~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}.$ ), Ni-BTC/NF ( $138.9~\mathrm{mV}$ $\operatorname{dec}^{-1}.$ ), $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P/N F}$ $:146.2~\mathrm{\mV~dec}^{-1}$ ), $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C}/$ NF-3 h $138.9\mathrm{~mV}$ $\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ ), $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@\cal C/\Nu\mathrm{F}{-}6$ h $\mathrm{^{\prime}}106.8\mathrm{\mV\dec^{-1}}.$ ) and $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-24\ h$ $(69.6~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}.$ ). The smallest Tafel slope indicates that $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}@\mathsf{C}/$
NF-12 h can easily overcome the kinetic process of OER. To reflect the OER catalytic kinetics more realistically, the Tafel slope was calculated using the constant potential steady state polarization method, as the HER test method.
The interval potential range of its chronoamperometric (CA) response is $-0.026\sim-0.476\:\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE. The steady-state OER polarization curve and Tafel slope are shown in the Fig. S2a-c. According to this method, we tested the OER LSV curve of the prepared material with the steady state polarization method, and the calculated result of the steady state Tafel slope as shown in Fig. 5e-f. The law of Tafel slope of the materials prepared at different growth times is basically equivalent, further confirming that ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P@C/N F-}}$ $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ showed excellent OER reaction kinetics. Compared with the other samples, ${\sf N i}_{2}{\sf P@C/N F-12}{\sf h}$ possesses the smallest charge transfer resistance $\mathrm{{Rct}}=2.7$ ohm), which is superior to other materials $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{NF}}=9.5$ ohm, $\mathrm{R_{Ni-BTC/NF}}=9.0$ ohm, $\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{Ni2P/NF}}=6.8$ ohm, RNi2P@C/NF-3h $\mathit{\Theta}=\mathit{\Theta}6.0$ ohm, RNi2P@C/NF-6h $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}4.2$ ohm, RNi2P@C/NF$_{24\mathrm{h}}=3.1$ ohm,), indicating that the MOF-derived $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-12\ h$ microspheres accelerate electron transfer during electrocatalysis (Fig. 5d). Long-term stability is another important indicator to evaluate whether the catalyst has the potential for industrial application. The OER electrochemical stability of ${\sf N i}_{2}{\sf P@C/N F}$ was evaluated by cyclic voltammetry (CV) and chronoamperometric curve measurements. As shown in Fig. 5e, the $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ current density of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ did not decay significantly under $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ of chronoamperometric operation. Furthermore, the loss of OER LSV polarization curves of $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-12\ h$ is negligible after 3000 CV cycles at a scan rate of $100\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ (Fig. 5f). The SEM (Figure S14) and
TEM (Figure S15) images of samples after CV test show that the nanospheres/nanoflowers structure remains unchanged. Raman spectrum test results show that in addition to the $\mathrm{I_{D}}$ and $\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{G}}$ peaks of carbon, the NiOOH peaks also appear at the $472~\mathrm{\cm}^{-1}$ , $554~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , proving that the new bonds formed by local oxidation on the surface are the real active substances of OER (Figure S16). XPS test was carried out on the materials after OER reaction. ${\mathsf{N i}}^{0}$ disappeared in $\mathsf{N i}_{2\mathsf{p}}$ while the characteristic peak of NiOOH appeared, which further verified the existence of NiOOH active site (Figure S17a). Surprisingly, it was found that the characteristic peaks of $\mathtt{P2p}$ disappeared after the reaction, also fully demonstrating the transition from ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ to NiOOH (Figure S17b). These results confirm that the material possesses excellent CV stability.
## 3.4. Optimization of preparation conditions
Electrocatalytic-activity-specific surface area (ECSA) is a key index reflecting the intrinsic activity of the $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ electrode material. Under these circumstances, cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements with RHE at $0.525\sim0.625\:\mathrm{V}$ at scan rates augmenting from 50 to $500\mathrm{mV}\thinspace s^{-1}$ in the Faradaic range were employed to obtain the double layer capacitance $\left(\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)$ (Figure S3). nickel foam, $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P/N F}$ , Ni-BTC/NF, $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-3\ h$ , $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\mathrm{P@C/NF-6^{\prime}~}$ h, ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P@C/N F-}}$ $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ and $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F-}24\mathrm{~h~}$ were calculated to be 1.1, 1.6, 2.3, 3.0, 5.1 and $6.7\:\mathrm{\mF}\:\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, indicating that ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P@C/N F-}}$ $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ and $24\mathrm{~h~}$ possesses the high ECSA and exposes more active sites (Figure S4). The larger $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}$ demonstrates that the dual synergy of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ and carbon skeleton exposes more active sites, which is beneficial to the performance of water splitting.
In order to further estimate the intrinsic catalytic activity of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ , the turnover frequency (TOF) for HER and OER was investigated. The relationships between the hydrogen and oxygen generation rate and the number of active sites through the CV test are shown in Figures S5 and S7. Compared with the other catalysts, $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\tt P@C/N F-12\ h$ revealed a high TOF in HER $(17.8~s^{-1}\$ and OER $\cdot15.2\ s^{-1},$ at an $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}$ of $300~\mathrm{{mV}}$ , much higher than that of the samples with other growth times (3 h, 6 h, 12 h and $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , NiBTC/NF, $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P/N F}$ (Figures S6 and S8).
In addition, we also optimized the material for the amount of surfactant PVP (0, 1, 2, 3, 4 g). As shown in Figures S9, ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P@C}}$ formed by $\textbf{1g}\mathsf{P V P}$ presents incomplete crystal nuclei, while it can basically form complete nanospheres under the conditions of $_{\textrm{2g}}$ PVP. The conditions of $_{\mathrm{~3~g~}}$ PVP can form extremely regular nanoflower-shaped spheres, while the nanospheres show excessive growth and agglomeration when $4\:\mathrm{g}\:\mathrm{PVP}$ was added. The catalyst prepared under the conditions of the addition of $_{\mathrm{~3~g~}}$ PVP shows an integrated morphology of nanoflowers and nanospheres, in which the convex nanospheres can fully contact the electrolyte, while the nanoflowers show a lamellar structure and expose more active sites. Therefore, PVP as a surfactant plays an important role in regulating the morphology of Ni-BTC crystals [51]. The performance of Ni-BTC derived material $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ containing PVP surfactant is significantly improved compared with that without PVP (Figures S10a-b, S11a-b). This multi-dimensional interface is extremely beneficial to the HER and OER catalytic performance of the catalyst. As we predict, the $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ catalyst of $3\mathrm{gPVP}$ exhibits relative to the lowest impedance and Tafel slope and excellent hydrogen and oxygen evolution performance compared with the others PVP additions (Figures S10, S11).
In summary, $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\mathrm{P@C/NF-12\h/3g}$ displays competitive overvoltage of HER and OER, its high intrinsic catalytic kinetics (lowest Tafel slope), large number of catalytically active sites (largest ECSA) and fast charge transfer ability (minimum Rct), demonstrated the advantages of MOF-derived carbon backbone and nanoflowers/nanospheres multi-dimensional interface, which benefited the rapid electron collection and transport during electrocatalysis.
## 3.5. Evaluation of overall water splitting
On account of the outstanding catalytic activity for both HER and OER in $1.0\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH aqueous solution, the performance of OWS was further appraised by using $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ assembled as anode and cathode in a two-electrode system. As a comparison, the cathode and anode $\begin{array}{r}{(\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}||\mathsf{R u O}_{2};}\end{array}$ were assembled with commercial $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf C/\mathsf{N F}$ and $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}/\mathsf{N F}$ , respectively under the same conditions. The method of $\Delta\mathsf{V}$ $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{U}=\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{\Omega}}$ , $\mathrm{HER}+\mathrm{V}_{\upeta}$ , OER) was adopted to evaluate the full performance of the HER and OER bifunctional catalysts. As shown as Fig. 6a, the $\Delta\mathsf{V}$ values of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ are 1. $50\ \vee\ @10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and $1.74~{\mathrm{V@100}}~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively. For the actual overall electrochemical evaluation of water splitting, the assembly system of $\mathrm{Ni_{2}P@C/N F||N i_{2}P@C/N F}$ can drive $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ and $100\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ current density when the cell voltage is only $1.55\mathrm{V}$ and $1.76\mathrm{V},$ , which is superior to that of commercial $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{N F}||\mathsf{R u O}_{2}/\mathsf{N F}$ at high current densities $(1.84\mathrm{V@100~mA~cm^{-2}})$ )
(Fig. 6b). Importantly, the water-splitting voltage of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F||}$ $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ is smaller than that of most reported self-supporting materials in $1.0~\mathsf{K O H}$ (Table S3). Meanwhile, it can be seen from Fig. 6c that $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C/N F}$ can maintain electrochemical stability for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . In addition, the volume of $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generated by the electrode materials was collected by continuous drainage in H-type electrolytic water (Figure S18). Fig. 6d shows the hydrogen (or oxygen) levels produced within $10\ \mathrm{min}$ of gas collection. The volume ratio of $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is close to 2:1, which is completely consistent with the theoretical values, illustrating that the Faradaic efficiency is close to $100\%$ to achieve water splitting. Overall, these results clearly indicate that $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P@C}/$ NF-12 is indeed an efficient and durable water splitting catalyst.
## 3.6. Analysis of electrocatalytic mechanism
## 4. Conclusion
Although the application of nickel phosphide in the catalytic mechanism of electrolytic water has made extensive research progress[45,48–57], there is few research on the effect of multidimensional interface regulation on active sites. Here, according to Ostwald Ripening theory[58,59], we have appropriately studied in depth the process of crystal nucleus growth to mature to control the multi-dimensional interface of electrode materials, which plays an important role in electron transfer and gas mass transfer, and is conducive to promoting the electrocatalytic process. This work not only deepens the application of Ostwald Ripening phenomena in electrolytic OWS but also clarifies the mechanism of significant bifunctional activity of synergistic effect between active components. What’s more, it provides guidance for the rational design of high-efficiency non-noble electrocatalysts based on multiphase interface engineering.
